; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED -- the simulated twin of the Surface Preparing clip: a chrome grab
; bar bolted into two black blocks on a gridded work mat, a blue marker stripe
; drawn along its crown, and a white kitchen sponge on the mat past the near
; block. The right arm takes the sponge, presses it onto the bar and wipes the
; stripe off along the bar's length while the left arm steadies the bar at its
; far elbow; then the sponge goes back where it came from.
;
; WHAT MAKES THIS ONE INTERESTING, and it is not the same thing as polish. There
; the tool is fixed and every mark has to be carried to it. Here the ABRASIVE is
; the thing that is carried: a block of foam picked up off the mat, taken to the
; work, and put back. The bar is a FIXTURE with a handle -- the real one is
; bolted into its blocks and does not leave the bench -- so nothing but the bar
; reacts the press, and the whole wipe force goes into the foam's wrap round the
; tube. (It was a free 22 mm bar with end cubes on two rest pads for the whole
; of the suite's earlier life, laid out so the support arm COULD lift it; one
; hand at one end never got it airborne -- assets/prep/PROVENANCE.md -- and the
; clip's bar is not lifted either.)
;
; AND THE ABRASIVE IS FOAM, WHICH IS WHY THE BAR IS ROUND. A rigid pad on a
; cylinder touches a line. Foam wraps it and touches an ARC, and how much of an
; arc is a function of how hard it is pressed -- measured on the 22 mm
; calibration pair with rgbs/scripts/rgbs_prep_bench.py, reading the
; co-simulation's own per-sample contacts:
;
;     overdrive   0 mm    1     2     3     4     6     8
;     force       0.00 N  0.09  0.46  1.06  1.82  3.60  5.64
;     wrap        43 deg  53    73    87    95    112   126
;
; The stripe is a 4 mm marker line on the crown, so reach round the tube is not
; what this variant asks for. What it asks for is TRAVEL -- the stripe is 200 mm
; long against a 75 mm sponge -- and a real press: the field's pressure floor
; cuts the feathered edge of a light one, and the working hand leans 35 degrees
; along the bar as the real one does, which lifts the sponge's far edge so the
; contact patch trails the hand.
;
; THE PRESS IS A THRESHOLD BEFORE IT IS A DIAL, and for a reason that is an
; artefact of nothing. SuperDex's penalty contact carries a padding band, so at
; zero overdrive there are already 292 contact samples spanning 43 degrees of
; the bar carrying 0.002 N between them. The field's pressure floor is what
; stops a policy cleaning the bar by holding the sponge near it.
;
; Removal is a per-cell irreversible Preston-law field (rate = k_p * pressure *
; sliding speed) owned by the `taint` entity, with pressure MEASURED -- each
; contact sample carries its own quadrature area -- rather than divided by a
; stated nominal patch. Nothing here can un-wipe a cell. The law is isotropic
; and says nothing about direction: what the stripe's cells see is decided
; entirely by whether the foam has reached them.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem WIPE_THE_BAR_CLEAN)


  (:language pick up the sponge, wipe the marker stripe off the grab bar with it,
             and put the sponge back down where it came from)

  ; The cell, the pairing (right wipes, left steadies, arms solid), the
  ; control mode and the episode count are rgbs.suite's; this is the delta.
  (:staging
    ; Two engines. The arms, the bar and the blocks are Newton's through
    ; MuJoCo, the sponge is SuperDex's on a host island. See rgbs/tasks/rgbs/staging.py.
    (:physics surface_prep_superdex@v1)
    ; The suite rig, aimed at the bar's crown mid-run -- the air the wipe
    ; happens in. The overhead cannot hold the whole 404 mm bar and the sponge
    ; past its block at this aim (the blocks sit at 0.77 of its half-frame, the
    ; sponge past the edge); the top, photo and inset views hold everything.
    (:cameras prep.layout:WIPE_VIEWS)
    ; Multi-phase: steady the bar, take the sponge, press and three out-and-
    ; back stroke cycles with a re-seat at the far end, set it back down,
    ; retreat both hands, and a two second settled hold. Generous on purpose:
    ; the shipped routine finishes well inside this, and the budget is sized
    ; for the strokes a lighter-pressing or worse-aimed one needs. ; CAL
    (:max-steps 12000)
    ; polish's and box_decant's stiffened servos, for the same reason:
    ; pressing a part against a compliant tool at a commanded force is
    ; task-space positioning under contact, and at the MJCF's own 300 gain
    ; the TCP lags ~10 mm -- more than the whole press range the routine's
    ; force loop works in.
    (:arm-actuation (:ke 1600) (:kd 70))
    (:gripper-actuation (:ke 20000) (:kd 20))
  )

  (:objects
    ; A welded object: it has a handle (the goal reads hands-clear off it and
    ; the routine reads its pose) and it never moves. Placed by its record.
    bar - grab_bar
    ; The stripe: state the BAR CARRIES, published by the task's taint field
    ; entity, the way polish's marks publish and peel's film publishes its bond
    ; field. No rigid body answers to this name and no clause below reads it
    ; through one -- published states only.
    taint - prep_taint
    ; Declared here so the goal's references resolve. It is not an object
    ; handle: it is a block of foam on a foreign engine.
    sponge - prep_sponge
  )

  ; Placed by their records: where the mat and the blocks stand is
  ; prep/layout.py's arithmetic, the clip's measurements written down.
  ; Two block records rather than one `bar_block`, because a record that
  ; builds a thing builds it in one place -- each stands under the leg the bar
  ; spec drops at its end.
  (:fixtures
    mat        - prep_mat
    block_near - prep_block_near
    block_far  - prep_block_far
  )

  ; No regions: the set-down is scored on the sponge being off the bar, still,
  ; and out of both hands, not on a box in space.

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; The world an episode starts in, re-verified after settling at every reset.
  ;
  ; Only the taint is stated, and that is deliberate. A field read is true at
  ; the reset instant. The two obvious neighbours are not, and both were
  ; measured false at reset and true a dozen steps later in the two sibling
  ; suites that tried them:
  ; * a contact-force clause -- the force atom smooths over ten steps and its
  ;   window is empty at verification, so it reads a dropout as zero
  ; * anything jaw-based -- the stall test needs a standing position error that
  ;   does not exist before the first control step
  ; Both quantities stay observable every step below.
  (:init
    (tainted taint)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; ONE LATCH OVER EVERYTHING: the stripe clean AND the sponge put down -- off
  ; the bar, still, out of both hands -- held for two simulated seconds so a
  ; bounce or a lingering finger cannot buy the verdict.
  ;
  ; (>= (cleaned-fraction taint) 0.95)
  ;     The field is monotone by construction, so the latch is belt-and-braces
  ;     -- but `once` is still right: the verdict is about the episode.
  ;
  ; (not (sponge-on-bar taint))
  ;     No part of the contact patch is pressing above the removal floor: the
  ;     sponge is off the bar. Without this the clock could start during the
  ;     routine's re-seat at the far end of the stripe, where the sponge inches
  ;     down a millimetre at a time and the hand sits just outside the bar's
  ;     hands-clear sphere.
  ;
  ; (< (sponge-speed taint) 0.02)
  ;     The sponge is still. Published by the field off the node positions the
  ;     co-simulation bridge writes back every control step, because the
  ;     library's own instrument, next line, cannot see a host-island body.
  ;
  ; (< (settle-speed sponge) 0.02)
  ;     The library's particle measure. It reads 0.0 for this sponge for the
  ;     whole episode -- a carry at 0.1 m/s included -- because the host island
  ;     never writes Newton's particle velocities. Kept so the clause reads the
  ;     way every other deformable's does and starts holding the day the
  ;     instrument works; the line above is what holds the verdict off today.
  ;
  ; (> (hands-clear bar) 0.10)
  ;     BOTH hands away from the bar -- `tcp-distance` reads the work arm
  ;     only, and this episode ends with two hands to clear. Measured from the
  ;     bar's centre, so the steadying hand at the far elbow (170 mm along)
  ;     reads clear even while it holds; the clause is about the wiping hand.
  ;     100 mm against a 94 mm jaw span. ; CAL
  (:goal
    (once
      (And
        (>= (cleaned-fraction taint) 0.95)
        (hold-for 2.0
          (And
            (not (sponge-on-bar taint))
            (< (sponge-speed taint) 0.02)
            (< (settle-speed sponge) 0.02)
            (> (hands-clear bar) 0.10)
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Progress toward the cleaning gate, clipped, UNMASKED -- polish's form. The
  ; set-down clauses are deliberately NOT folded into the scalar: reward
  ; measures how much got cleaned, and release is a condition on when the
  ; verdict counts. A wipe-force budget belongs in a later gentle variant,
  ; after the bench has measured clean-run and abusive-run populations -- an
  ; uncalibrated threshold is worth less than the number it thresholds.
  (:reward (progress (cleaned-fraction taint) (:gate 0.95)))

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Recorded every step, gating nothing -- the quantities this suite exists to
  ; calibrate. `wipe-force` is the future gentle-variant budget, `slide-speed`
  ; is what separates a wipe from a stamp, `sponge-speed` beside `settle-speed`
  ; is the live instrument beside the dead one, and `patches-remaining` reads
  ; 1 until the stripe is nine tenths gone.
  (:observe
    (patches-remaining taint)
    (wipe-force taint)
    (slide-speed taint)
    (sponge-on-bar taint)
    (sponge-speed taint)
    (settle-speed sponge)
    (hands-clear bar)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; The privileged sim.* surface, and the scripted routine that spends it. No
  ; graph yet: the routine IS the reference solution here, the way box_decant's
  ; and cable_route's are, and it is what `--solve routine` drives. The same
  ; choreography plays on the live page (rgbs/scripts/surface_cleaning.py).
  (:artifacts
    (:tools "tools:register_surface_prep_tools")
    (:routine "routine:RoutineSurfacePrep")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier original)
    (:source "Authored for this corpus from the Surface Preparing clip of the real
              rig, measured off its frames and its overhead inset: a 25 mm
              chrome grab bar with a 340 mm straight run on two elbows, its
              legs dropping 50 mm into two 76 x 80 x 70 mm black blocks, on a
              10 mm interlocking mat with a 25 mm grid on 300 mm tiles; a
              50 x 75 x 30 mm kitchen sponge on the mat 70 mm past the near
              block and 35 mm beyond the bar's line; a marker stripe along the
              middle 200 mm of the crown. The bar is welded to the world, as
              the real one is bolted to its blocks. The live page's twin of
              the same clip (rgbs/scripts/surface_cleaning.py) builds this
              scene from these records and plays this routine.")
    (:note "Stripe 200 mm by 9 degrees of half-angle -- a ~4 mm line -- on 2.5 mm
            cells, 243 of them, LONGER ALONG THE BAR THAN THE SPONGE IS, which
            is what makes the wipe travel. Shorter than the photograph's stripe
            on purpose: the working hand's far stroke stays a jaw's width clear
            of the hand steadying the far elbow, and a stroke to the end of a
            longer stripe shoved that hand off. Removal is a per-cell
            irreversible field, so cleaned-fraction is monotone and `once`
            latches nothing the world could take back. Pressure is the
            engine's own per-sample force over its own quadrature area, so
            unlike polish there is no stated nominal patch for k_p to be
            fitted against and drift from. The shipped routine presses by
            force -- descending a millimetre at a time until the field reads
            4 N -- because the leaning hand puts the sponge's lowest point
            somewhere no flat-grasp measurement predicts, and trims the seat
            height between stroke segments because the arm sags differently
            along the bar and the pinned foam creeps in the jaws. CAL-tagged
            thresholds are re-measured against the shipped policy before
            sign-off.")
  )

  ; THE ARENA YAM, as a second way to stand this variant. `yam_bimanual` is the
  ; same I2RT arm as the native pairing -- 264 mm upper arm, 245 mm forearm, the
  ; same joint ranges -- wearing the arena's `yam_linear_arena` hand instead of
  ; the `linear_4310` blades, and standing 22 mm higher above the work surface
  ; because that robot's mount clears its own play table.
  ;
  ; Stated per variant rather than on the suite, because the suite's members do
  ; not agree on which arm works: `handle`, `panel` and `panel_gentle` override
  ; the suite's `right` with `left`, and one suite-level row would claim the
  ; wrong arm for three documents and collide with the row `handle` already
  ; carries.
  ;
  ; What it does NOT claim: the two hands share no tool point -- the i2rt grasp
  ; site is 134.7 mm down the gripper body's own axis, the arena's is 130 mm out
  ; and 44 mm laterally off the wrist axis, with 12.14 mm of tip reach against
  ; 35 mm -- so every constant fitted to the blades is a fact about that hand.
  ; A row says how to stand the robot here, never that the pairing works.
  ;
  ; Measured 2026-09-06 by scripts/matrix.py: built, stepped 10 control steps
  ; finite, work arm within HOME_ERR of its declared home.
  (:hosts
    (:host (:embodiment yam_bimanual (:work-arm right) (:support-arm left) (:arms-solid true))
           (:notes "compiles; no policy"))
  )
)
